  "name": "Question 17",
  "problem": "An object of mass $25\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v$ be its velocity, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the object is proportional to the square of its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k=5\\,\\text{kg/m}$ and acts opposite to the direction of motion.\n\nWhat is the terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}}$ of the object? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the object are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\n${v}_{\\text{term}}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}$",
  "worked_solution": "Terminal velocity is the constant speed a falling object approaches as the upward resistive force (drag) grows to exactly balance the downward gravitational force.\nThat is, at terminal velocity (a constant), acceleration is zero, so by $F=ma,$ the resultant force is zero.\n\nFirst, note that only two forces act on the object: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=25\\cdot 9.8=245\\,\\text{N},\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the square of the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-k{v}^{2}=-5{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the object is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=245-5{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the object reaches terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}},$ the resultant force is zero:\n\n$$\n245-5({v}_{\\text{term}})^{2}=0\\,\u27f9\\,{v}_{\\text{term}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{245}{5}}=7\n$$\n\nTherefore, the terminal velocity of the object is ${v}_{\\text{term}}=7\\,\\text{m/s}.$",
